.TH DHCLIENT 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
dhclient \- DHCPv4 client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dhclient
.I interface
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dhclient
obtains an IPv4 address lease for
.I interface
from a DHCP server, installs it on the interface, and keeps it by
renewing it before it expires.
It takes exactly one argument, the interface name; there are no options.
.PP
The client first reads the interface's hardware address and index.
It then broadcasts a DHCPDISCOVER from 0.0.0.0 port 68 to
255.255.255.255 port 67 over an
.B AF_PACKET
socket, since the interface may not have an address yet.
Up to four DISCOVERs are sent, waiting about 4, 8, 16 and 32 seconds
(each randomized by up to one second) for an offer: about a minute in all.
An offer is accepted only if it carries a server identifier and offers
an address a host can use (not 0/8, 127/8, multicast, class E or the
limited broadcast address).
.PP
The client then broadcasts a DHCPREQUEST for the offered address,
retransmitted up to four times with the same backoff.
A DHCPNAK, or no answer, sends the client back to the start with a new
transaction ID; this is tried three times in all before
.B dhclient
gives up.
An acknowledgement from a server other than the one selected is ignored.
.PP
Before using an acknowledged address,
.B dhclient
sends two ARP probes for it (sender address 0.0.0.0) and listens for one
second.
If another host answers for the address, or is probing for it, the client
sends a DHCPDECLINE, waits ten seconds and starts over.
.PP
The lease is taken from the DHCPACK: the address
.RI ( yiaddr ),
the subnet mask (option 1) and the first router (option 3).
If the server sends no mask, the classful mask of the address is used;
if it sends no router, the interface's gateway is cleared.
These are installed with the
.BR SIOCSIFADDR ,
.B SIOCSIFNETMASK
and
.B SIOCSIFGATEWAY
ioctls, and the address is announced with a gratuitous ARP reply.
If any of the ioctls fails, the lease is not reported as bound and
.B dhclient
exits with status 1.
.PP
If the acknowledgement carries DNS servers (option 6), a domain name
(option 15) or a domain search list (option 119),
.B dhclient
rewrites
.I /etc/resolv.conf
with
.BR nameserver ,
.B domain
and
.B search
lines, by writing
.I /etc/resolv.conf.new
and renaming it into place.
A domain name, or a search-list entry, that contains anything other than
letters, digits, hyphens and dots is dropped.
If none of these options is present,
.I /etc/resolv.conf
is left alone.
.PP
Requests carry a parameter request list (options 1, 3, 6, 15, 119 and 51)
and a maximum message size of 1500 octets.
If the system hostname is set (read from
.IR /etc/hostname ,
otherwise
.BR gethostname (2)),
and is not empty,
.B localhost
or
.BR (none) ,
it is sent as the host name (option 12, truncated to 63 bytes) and, prefixed
by a zero type byte, as the client identifier (option 61).
The BROADCAST flag is left clear.
.SS Keeping the lease
If the lease is infinite (lease time 0xffffffff, or no lease time given),
.B dhclient
prints a message and exits 0 once bound.
Otherwise it forks: the parent exits 0 so that the caller (typically boot)
continues, and the child calls
.BR setsid (2)
and stays in the background to maintain the lease.
.PP
The renewal time T1 and rebinding time T2 come from options 58 and 59,
or default to 0.5 and 0.875 of the lease; each is randomized by up to 5%.
At T1 the child unicasts DHCPREQUESTs to the leasing server (RENEWING);
from T2 it broadcasts them (REBINDING) until the lease ends.
These go through an ordinary UDP socket on port 68.
After each request it waits half of the time remaining in the phase, but
at least 60 seconds.
An acknowledgement updates the lease and reinstalls it (and
.IR /etc/resolv.conf ).
.PP
On a DHCPNAK, or when the lease expires, the child removes the address and
gateway from the interface and starts again from DHCPDISCOVER, retrying
every 60 seconds until it gets a new lease.
.PP
Progress messages are written to standard output and errors to standard
error; the background child keeps the descriptors it inherited.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A lease was bound and installed (the background child, if any, continues),
or the fork failed after binding, in which case the lease will not be
renewed.
.TP
.B 1
No lease was obtained, the lease could not be installed, or the interface
could not be queried or its sockets set up.
.TP
.B 2
Wrong number of arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/resolv.conf
Resolver configuration, rewritten when the server supplies DNS options.
.TP
.I /etc/hostname
Source of the host name and client identifier sent to the server.
.TP
.I /etc/network.conf
Interfaces with the mode
.B dhcp
are passed to
.B dhclient
by
.IR /etc/rc.d/20-network
at boot.
.SH EXAMPLE
Lease an address for the first Ethernet interface, as the boot scripts do
for an
.B "eth0 dhcp"
line in
.IR /etc/network.conf :
.EX
# dhclient eth0
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ifconfig (8),
.BR radvd (8),
.BR init (8),
.BR resolv.conf (5)
.SH STANDARDS
RFC 2131 (DHCP), RFC 2132 (DHCP options), RFC 3397 (domain search option).
Conformance notes are in
.I docs/dhclient\-audit\-2026\-09.md
in the source tree.
.SH BUGS
There is no way to release a lease: the daemon does not handle signals and
never sends DHCPRELEASE, and it writes no pid file.
.PP
Only IPv4 is supported; there is no DHCPv6.
.PP
At most 16 name servers are written to
.IR /etc/resolv.conf .
